[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: The strictdoc-tooling plan's backlog register

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-BACKLOG-STRICTDOC-TOOLING
TITLE: The strictdoc-tooling plan's backlog register
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The linchpin every ungroomed item in this plan points at. An item is in the
backlog when it carries Backlogged_In to this node, and the plan's backlog is
the inbound Backlogs set.

This node holds no list. The edges are the list, which is what lets an item sit
in its own file, carry its own other edges, and still be findable as backlog
without anything scanning a directory.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Containment used to be by file: a 99-backlog document held the items, so opening
it was how you saw them. The one-node-per-file carve dissolved that file and
left the ungroomed set as a DEPTH == sketch query over a directory, which works
but is not part of the graph -- it is a fact about where bytes sit, invisible to
anything traversing edges.

DEC-PLAN-SCOPED-BACKLOG anticipated exactly this and named the successor in its
own retirement condition: an item needing to be tracked while living outside the
plan's backlog document is the point at which an explicit register node and a
Backlogged_In role become the cheaper answer. The carve made every item live
outside it, so the condition fired.

An edge also composes where a file did not. An item can be Backlogged_In here
AND carry Governed_By, Assumes or Crosses to whatever it actually relates to,
which is the web the graph is for. A file forced one containment and a directory
query forces one more.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Created 2026-08-27 on the operator's instruction, after the carve: "having the
99 is fine, but its the lynchpin node. every issue under it parents that. issues
may parent other nodes that are related, but thats the idea, a web."

Deliberately thin. Full item semantics -- what kinds there are, what grooming
promotes, what closes -- are milestone five's, not this node's. This is the
intermediate shape that makes one-node-per-file work without deciding any of it.

The DEPTH == sketch query still answers and is worth keeping as a CROSS-CHECK:
an item at sketch with no Backlogged_In edge is one somebody filed without
attaching, and an item carrying the edge at a higher DEPTH is one that was
groomed without being detached. Neither is enforced yet;
MECH-SDOC-LAYOUT-CHECK is the natural home for both once it exists.
<<<
